 "name": "American soft power in East Asia and the Pacific: how policymakers assessed it",
 "authorLine": "Agent run f1, 2026-10-31",
 "introductionText": "What FRUS answers directly: across 22 documents from 1950 to 1977, policymakers judged America's standing in East Asia and the Pacific mostly as a matter of credibility - whether Asians believed U.S. commitments and saw American strength, reliability and friendship - and they built information programs aimed at elites, run increasingly through Asian governments. They doubted their own measures of effect, and their beliefs about the audience ranged from 'meager knowledge' of the United States (1950) to race-sensitivity, colonial suspicion and 'America watchers' (1969). Where FRUS points for the rest: the documents came mostly from USIA's own files in Record Group 306 (Director's Subject Files, Entry A1-42, and earlier director's files), presidential library national security files, and State's Executive Secretariat NSC policy-paper files (Lot 63 D 351/66 D 95, NAID 2839192); the editors also cite unprinted Central Decimal File class 511 (U.S. informational relations), OCB files (Lot 61 D 385) and SEA files (Lot 58 D 726, NAID 2544398). What this request cannot get from FRUS: the phrase 'soft power' and the word 'messaging' do not occur as such (both postdate the period); USIA's audience survey reports themselves are not printed; and no Asian audience speaks for itself here - reception is only seen through American reporting.",
